# Summary
A lifecycle expiration path was added that calls permanent delete / delete-marker helpers without validating the live object's Object Lock state. This bypasses existing delete protections enforced elsewhere in the delete API.
The background lifecycle sweeper evaluates expiration and then directly deletes current live objects or inserts delete markers without calling the Object Lock validation logic. This means objects under legal hold or retention can be expired and removed by lifecycle rules, bypassing WORM-style protections. A bucket owner (or attacker with equivalent privileges) can configure lifecycle rules to delete data that should be immutable, violating retention guarantees and potentially causing compliance-impacting data loss.

# Reviewer note
Reviewed against commit `a09a766` and current tree behavior. This does not appear to be a valid security issue.

- In Object Lock buckets, lifecycle expiration of the current version in a versioned bucket creates a delete marker instead of permanently deleting the retained version. That matches the existing `DELETE` without `versionId` behavior and AWS Object Lock semantics.
- Object Lock buckets cannot be moved to `Suspended` or `Disabled` versioning, so the report's null-version/suspended path is unreachable for valid Object Lock buckets.
- Non-Object-Lock buckets cannot store object-lock state on writes, so the nonversioned permanent-delete path is not a retained-object bypass either.

Relevant code paths:
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:2557-2637` lifecycle current-expiration path
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:5524-5542` rejects suspending/disabling versioning on Object Lock buckets
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:10734-10768` normal delete without `versionId` inserts a delete marker
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:24890-24919` test coverage showing retained current versions allow delete-marker creation but reject direct version deletion
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator.rs:6518-6552` rejects object-lock state on plain buckets

AWS references:
- Object Lock considerations: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock-managing.html
  This documents that after enabling Object Lock on a bucket, you cannot suspend versioning for that bucket.
- Expiring objects: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/lifecycle-expire-general-considerations.html
  This documents that lifecycle `Expiration` in versioning-enabled buckets adds a delete marker, and in versioning-suspended buckets creates a null-version delete marker.
- Locking objects with Object Lock: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/object-lock.html
  This documents that simple `DELETE` without `versionId` on a protected object returns success and inserts a delete marker, while permanent `DELETE` with `versionId` is denied.
- Troubleshooting Amazon S3 Lifecycle issues: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/troubleshoot-lifecycle.html
  This documents that for objects protected by Object Lock, current versions are not permanently deleted by lifecycle expiration and noncurrent locked versions are preserved.

