## Report
Crash attempt: `cargo test -p server-core lifecycle_sweep_expires_nonversioned_current_object -- --nocapture` failed to build because `ec-sys` requires `libisal` (build script panicked: “libisal not found; install intel-isa-l or set PKG_CONFIG_PATH”). Valgrind attempt: `valgrind --version` not found. Debugger attempt: `lldb -b -o "run" -o "bt" -- cargo test ...` stopped at exec and could not proceed to a runnable target; build is blocked by missing libisal.

Code evidence: Lifecycle sweep expiration deletes or hides live objects without Object Lock validation. `expire_current_object_if_due` loads a live record and, once expired, directly calls `permanently_delete_live_object_locked`, `put_delete_marker_locked`, or `expire_current_live_suspended_locked` based on versioning (coordinator.rs:2557-2637). None of these calls validate `record.object_lock`. The deletion helper `permanently_delete_live_object_locked` removes metadata and schedules reclaim without any Object Lock check (coordinator.rs:9177-9245), and `expire_current_live_suspended_locked` inserts a delete marker and deletes stale payload metadata without Object Lock checks (coordinator.rs:9267-9295). In contrast, the normal delete path enforces Object Lock via `validate_delete_against_object_lock` (coordinator.rs:6636-6660) before deleting a live object (coordinator.rs:10637-10652). LiveObjectRecord stores `object_lock`, so the sweeper has access to it (storage/src/types.rs:976-1001) but does not use it. This confirms the lifecycle expiration path can bypass legal hold/retention protections.

